Z||000000||fropuff||06-07-2000||11:07 AM||||I was wondering if anyone knows whose great idea it was to put the battery in the rear?! Because I want to find them and have THEM fix my battery.  What happened is that the battery got wet from under the deck lid vents and now the battery is shot!  When I try to start it, it starts but sputters.  I have to pump the gas to keep it running.  When I let off, it sputters to a stop.  I have tried to charge it twice, and neither time has worked.  It also smells like it's burning after I try to start.  I could just strangle them!  What is wrong with my car?  The battery or worse?  Thanks.  How did they expect you to wash your car?  I wash mine almost every day now.  I'll have to get the kit to put it in the front of the car.  Thanks again.  ||||reg||8
Z||000001||Uaana||06-07-2000||11:28 AM||||Ah fropuff<BR>That doesn't sound like a bad battery sounds like a bad connection. If you have gotten water under your hood the first place to check is the coil connection. That is one of the weakest links in the starting system. and if you are running stock wires or wires that are over a year old, they can start to arc and short quite easily.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||06-07-2000||12:03 PM||||Coil Connection?  I know I'm not that smart, but where is this thing you call a coil connection?  I think the guy I bought it from had jsut replaced the wires, so they're good.  Thanks Uanna.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||06-07-2000||12:51 PM||||<BR>The easiest way to find the coil is to trace the wire coming from the middle of your ditributer cap.<P>However, if you have an 87 4cyl then you may not be able to that, because '87 and '88 have DIS.  But you may still find that coil mounted on the engine block to the left of the intake manifold.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||06-07-2000||08:30 PM||||had the same problem, but it wasn't from water.<P>Just a bad connection on the starter.  Check them all to make sure they are tight.||||reg||1
